.TH CYAML_CREATE_STRING_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_create_string_mp, cyaml_create_string \- allocate a YAML string node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_create_string_mp(const char *val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cyaml cyaml_create_string(const char *val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocate a DOM node of type CYAML_STRING.  The node holds its own copy
of val on the heap.  A NULL val gives a CYAML_NULL node.  A NULL val
therefore never makes this function return NULL.

val must be well-formed UTF-8.  The function refuses a string that is
not, and returns NULL.  It never rewrites such a string.  Every Unicode
character is accepted, including those that a YAML document may hold
only as an escape (a C0 control, DEL, a C1 control, U+FFFE and
U+FFFF): cyaml_serialize(3) writes a string that holds one
double-quoted, with an escape, so the output parses back to the same
text.
cyaml_create_string() passes NULL for mp.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
val  Null-terminated, well-formed UTF-8 source string, or NULL for a
     null node.
mp   Custom allocator, or NULL for default.
     The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller may
     release or reuse the struct once this call returns. The functions
     that it names must stay callable until the last node built with
     them is destroyed. A process can use at most 64 distinct sets of
     four functions; a call that would need a 65th fails and returns
     NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
New cyaml node on success.  NULL when val is not well-formed UTF-8,
when the allocation fails, or when the allocator table is full (see
mp).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_str_val (3),
.BR cyaml_destroy (3)
